 Targets can be `current`, `all`, `*`, a monitor connector name such as `eDP-1` or `DP-1`, or a monitor selector token. Values accept normalized `0.0` to `1.0` or percentage form such as `65`, `65%`, and `5%`.
 
+`brightness-list-backlight-devices` only lists raw `/sys/class/backlight` entries - it is not a health check. External-monitor-only rigs normally have no backlight devices there even when DDC/CI control works; use `brightness-status` to tell "no sysfs backlight" apart from "DDC/CI disabled or not yet discovered".
+
 `brightness-osd` takes a value only - unlike `brightness-set`, it has no monitor target. The OSD starts on the focused output when `[osd].follow_focused_output` is enabled and stays there until the popup disappears; otherwise it appears on whichever monitors are configured under `[osd].monitors`.
 
 ## Night light
diff --git a/docs/user/services/brightness.mdx b/docs/user/services/brightness.mdx
index d256e87c9..5daa1d35e 100644
--- a/docs/user/services/brightness.mdx
+++ b/docs/user/services/brightness.mdx
@@ -5,11 +5,13 @@ sidebar:
   order: 3
 ---
 
-Brightness control uses the kernel backlight interface by default. `ddcutil` support is opt-in for external monitors that expose DDC/CI brightness.
+Brightness control uses the kernel backlight interface when one exists. For external monitors that expose DDC/CI brightness but have no kernel backlight device, `ddcutil` discovery runs automatically - no opt-in required. Set `enable_ddcutil` to `true` or `false` to override that decision explicitly.
 
 ```toml
 [brightness]
-enable_ddcutil     = false
+# enable_ddcutil is unset by default = auto (DDC/CI is discovered for monitors without a kernel backlight)
+# enable_ddcutil   = true    # always try DDC/CI discovery
+# enable_ddcutil   = false   # never use DDC/CI, even for external monitors
 ignore_mmids       = []     # e.g. ["ACI-ROG_PG279Q-10220"] - skip in all ddcutil commands
 minimum_brightness = 0.0    # never let brightness drop below this floor (0.0 to 1.0)
 sync_all_monitors = false   # if set to true, makes every display have the same brightness
@@ -23,14 +25,15 @@ backend = "ddcutil"
 ```
 
 Notes:
-- `enable_ddcutil = true` only enables DDC/CI discovery - it does not force every monitor to `ddcutil`.
+- `enable_ddcutil` is tri-state: unset means auto, `true` always enables DDC/CI discovery, `false` always disables it. Enabling only turns discovery on - it does not force every monitor to `ddcutil`.
+- In auto mode, `ddcutil` is authoritative: any external monitor it reports over I2C becomes a controllable display, even when the compositor does not currently expose that connector as a Wayland output (useful in nested sessions and for hot-docked externals). Setting `enable_ddcutil` explicitly restores the original filter that requires the compositor to know the connector.
 - `ignore_mmids` passes `--ignore-mmid` to every `ddcutil` invocation. Run `ddcutil --verbose detect` to find monitor model id strings.
 - `minimum_brightness` is an opt-in floor (default `0.0` = disabled). Any brightness change is clamped to at least this value, and the control center slider cannot go below it - useful to keep the screen from going completely dark.
 - `sync_all_monitors` only syncs the brightnesses when one of the display brightnesses change, it does not actively sync them every now and then
 - Per-monitor overrides use the same connector/description matching rules as bar monitor overrides.
 - `backend = "auto"` prefers kernel backlight when available and falls back to `ddcutil`.
 - `backend = "none"` hides brightness control for the matched monitor.
-- `backlight_device` pins a monitor to a specific sysfs backlight device when a connector exposes more than one - accepts a bare name (`intel_backlight`) or a full path; run `noctalia msg brightness-list-backlight-devices` to list the available device names.
+- `backlight_device` pins a monitor to a specific sysfs backlight device when a connector exposes more than one - accepts a bare name (`intel_backlight`) or a full path; run `noctalia msg brightness-list-backlight-devices` to list the available device names. That command is a raw sysfs view, not a health check: external-monitor-only rigs normally have no backlight devices at all. Use `caeliglow msg brightness-status` to see which backend applies and what was discovered.
 - `ddcutil` is treated as best-effort - repeated DDC failures cool down that monitor to avoid hammering the bus.
 
 DDC/CI discovery runs in the background. If a monitor is not ready when Noctalia starts or outputs change,
@@ -39,9 +42,12 @@ Working brightness controls remain available while other monitors are being disc
 extend the total recovery time.
 
 If controls are still missing after those retries, open **Control Center → Monitor** and use **Rescan DDC/CI monitors**
-(the refresh icon) to start another discovery attempt and retry cycle. Changing brightness configuration also
-starts a new cycle. Outputs configured with `backend = "none"` or `backend = "backlight"`, and outputs already
-served by kernel backlight in `auto` mode, are excluded from DDC recovery.
+(the refresh icon) to start another discovery attempt and retry cycle - or do it from the CLI with
+`caeliglow msg brightness-rescan`, which re-runs `ddcutil detect` in the background and reports the candidate
+connectors. `caeliglow msg brightness-status` distinguishes "no sysfs backlight" from "DDC/CI disabled or not
+yet discovered". Changing brightness configuration also starts a new cycle. Outputs configured with
+`backend = "none"` or `backend = "backlight"`, and outputs already served by kernel backlight in `auto` mode,
+are excluded from DDC recovery.
 
